course: dictionary
lesson: 3986
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is right. Gall is a good example. «He had the gall to ask for more after eating mine!» Here gall means rude, bold cheek: he was not shy about asking, and that was rude.
explain: Look at the words around gall. Oak galls grow on trees, and gall bladders are inside bodies, so the sentence tells you which meaning to use. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it makes sense.
explain: Gall can be a noun or a verb. As a noun it can mean rude cheek, a lump on a plant, or an old word for bile. As a verb, to gall someone means to annoy them a lot because it feels unfair. A verb often shows an action, so look for something being done to someone.
explain: «An oak gall forms when a tiny wasp lays an egg in an oak bud and the tree grows a ball round it.» The clues are oak, wasp and egg, so gall here means the lump on the plant. When you meet a word with several meanings, a dictionary will list them all.
fact: Gall can be a noun or a verb.
fact: «Gall» means rude, bold cheek, as in «the gall to ask for more».
fact: A gall is a lump on a plant, such as an oak apple, made when an insect lays eggs in it.
fact: Old meaning: gall is bile, a bitter liquid from the liver, which the gall bladder stores.
fact: To gall someone means to annoy them a lot because it feels unfair.
exercise: «He had the gall to ask for more after eating mine!» What does gall mean here? | A lump on a plant | Kindness and care | *Rude, bold cheek | A bitter liquid in the body
exercise: What is an oak gall? | A tall branch on an oak tree | An acorn that falls in autumn | *A lump on a plant, made when a tiny insect lays eggs in it | A type of oak leaf
exercise: To gall someone means what? | *To annoy them a lot because it feels unfair | To give them a present | To ask them for something | To make them laugh
test: In body science, where does the gall bladder sit? | *Under the liver | In the head | On the skin | In the chest, near the heart
test: Long ago, what were oak galls used to make? | Paper | *Black ink | Candles | Soap
exam: Which sentence shows gall meaning rude, bold cheek? | Ink was made from oak galls | The gall bladder is under the liver | The oak gall grew big on the branch | *«He had the gall to ask for more after eating mine!»
